description:
In repos/development-vessel/src/resolvers/git-status.ts, make exactly ONE edit: replace the following block of lines with the block given below. This adds git-worktree support to the HEAD lookup.

REPLACE these lines, verbatim:
    const headRaw = await Bun.file(`${repoPath}/.git/HEAD`).text();
    const head = headRaw.trim();

    // `ref: refs/heads/<branch>` — resolve the ref the repo ACTUALLY points at rather than
    // assuming a branch name. The previous code inherited whatever ref HEAD named, which is
    // correct; what was wrong was reading it from the wrong repository.
    if (head.startsWith("ref: ")) {
      const ref = head.slice(5).trim();
      const refPath = `${repoPath}/.git/${ref}`;

WITH these lines, verbatim:
    // GIT WORKTREE INDIRECTION (2026-09-11). In a LINKED WORKTREE `.git` is a FILE
    // holding "gitdir: <path>", not a directory — so `${repoPath}/.git/HEAD` does not
    // exist, the read throws ENOENT into the catch below, and the returned body carries
    // an `error` and NO `commitHash`. Measured: isolated composes stage into a fresh
    // worktree, so this resolver failed for every one of them, and the vessel's own
    // integration test ("resolves git_status in the development-vessel repo itself")
    // then failed with `Received value must be a string: undefined` — rolling the
    // compose back and reporting a test name rather than the cause.
    let gitDir = `${repoPath}/.git`;
    try {
      const dotGitText = await Bun.file(gitDir).text();
      if (dotGitText.startsWith("gitdir:")) gitDir = dotGitText.slice("gitdir:".length).trim();
    } catch { /* .git is a directory (ordinary checkout) — keep the default */ }

    const headRaw = await Bun.file(`${gitDir}/HEAD`).text();
    const head = headRaw.trim();

    // `ref: refs/heads/<branch>` — resolve the ref the repo ACTUALLY points at rather than
    // assuming a branch name. The previous code inherited whatever ref HEAD named, which is
    // correct; what was wrong was reading it from the wrong repository.
    if (head.startsWith("ref: ")) {
      const ref = head.slice(5).trim();
      const refPath = `${gitDir}/${ref}`;

ANCHORING: the line `    const headRaw = await Bun.file(\`${repoPath}/.git/HEAD\`).text();` occurs EXACTLY ONCE in this file, inside `resolveGitStatus`. The two substantive changes are that `${repoPath}/.git` is replaced by a resolved `gitDir` in BOTH the HEAD read and the refPath construction, and that a small block resolving the worktree pointer is inserted above them.

WHY. `resolveGitStatus` treats `${repoPath}/.git` as a DIRECTORY. That holds for an ordinary checkout. It is FALSE in a linked git worktree, where `.git` is a FILE whose entire contents are a single line `gitdir: /absolute/path/to/the/real/git/dir`. In that case `${repoPath}/.git/HEAD` does not exist at all.

VERIFIED DIRECTLY, 2026-09-11, against a real worktree in this repository:
  .git contents: gitdir: /.../.git/modules/repos/development-vessel/worktrees/agent-a23929b53bdc18aea
  does <worktree>/.git/HEAD exist?  NO
  does <real gitdir>/HEAD exist?    YES

CONSEQUENCE, measured. The ENOENT is caught by the existing catch, which returns `{ shape: 'gitStatus', body: { error, repoPath } }` — a body with NO commitHash. The vessel's own integration test 'resolves git_status in the development-vessel repo itself' asserts `expect(b.commitHash).toMatch(/^[0-9a-f]{40}$/)` and therefore fails with the message 'Received value must be a string: undefined'. Isolated composes stage into a fresh worktree and run the suite there, so this fires for every isolated compose of this vessel: the change is rolled back and the reported reason names a test rather than the cause. Two operator dispatches were lost to exactly this today, and the first was misdiagnosed as a flake precisely because the test passes in an ordinary checkout, dirty or clean.

THE FIX. Read `.git` as text first. If it starts with 'gitdir:', the repository is a worktree and the remainder of that line is the real git directory, so use it. If reading as text throws — which is what happens when `.git` is an ordinary directory — keep the existing path unchanged.

SAFETY. For an ordinary checkout the catch fires immediately and `gitDir` keeps its original value `${repoPath}/.git`, so both reads are byte-identical to today's behaviour. The detached-HEAD branch below is untouched and still returns `head` directly. The outer try/catch and the error shape are unchanged, so a genuinely missing repository still returns the same shaped error. No signature, no type, and no other call site changes.

